description:
Build the reliability layer of a game protocol: one endpoint of a connection that sends a packet every tick, acknowledges the other side's packets with a bitfield, and carries reliable, ordered messages that are resent until they are known to have arrived. The tests connect two endpoints through a link that loses, delays and repeats packets, and check that every message arrives once and in order.

```cpp
class ReliableEndpoint
{
public:
    explicit ReliableEndpoint(double resendAfter);
    bool send(const std::string& message);
    std::vector<std::uint8_t> update(double now);
    void receive(const std::vector<std::uint8_t>& packet);
    std::vector<std::string> take();
    std::size_t unacked() const;
};
```

**The packet** (all numbers big-endian):

| Bytes | Field |
|---|---|
| 0-1 | sequence |
| 2-3 | ack |
| 4-7 | ack bits |
| 8 | message count |
| then, per message | message id (2 bytes), length (2 bytes), the bytes |

**Sending.** `send` queues a message, giving it the next message id (0, 1, 2 ..., wrapping at 65 536), and returns true; a message longer than 1 000 bytes is refused with false. `unacked` is the number of queued messages not yet acknowledged.

`update(now)` builds and returns exactly one packet, every time it is called, even with no messages in it:

- its sequence number is the next one (0 for the first packet, then 1, 2, ...);
- `ack` and `ack bits` describe the packets received from the other side, exactly as in practice 5.03; before any packet has been received they are 65 535 and 0 (the other side's numbers start at 0, so this acknowledges nothing);
- it then goes through the queue in id order and includes each message that has never been sent, or was last sent `resendAfter` or more before `now`. It stops at the first such message that would make the packet larger than 1 200 bytes, or when the packet already holds 255 messages. Each included message is marked as sent at `now`;
- it remembers which messages went into this sequence number.

**Receiving.** `receive` first checks the whole packet: at least 9 bytes, every message fits, and nothing is left over. A malformed packet is ignored completely. Otherwise:

1. Record its sequence number for your own acks (practice 5.03).
2. Read its `ack` and `ack bits`. Each of your packets they acknowledge for the first time is delivered: every message in it is acknowledged and leaves the queue (it may be in several packets; the first ack is enough).
3. Deliver its messages in order: keep the id of the next message to deliver (starting at 0). A message whose id is that one or up to 1 023 after it (by the signed difference of practice 5.02) is buffered if it is not already; anything else is a duplicate or nonsense and is dropped. Then, as long as the next id is buffered, move it to the delivered list and advance.

`take` returns the messages delivered since the last call, in order, and clears that list.

